  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:1",
      "id": "ex-nondedekind-ideal-not-invertible",
      "location": "Example",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The example writes only \"Let R:=k[t^2,t^3]\" but never assumes that k is a field; step 1.1 then calls k[t] \"a one-variable polynomial ring over a field.\" Taking k=Z gives R=Z[t^2,t^3], integral over Z[t^2] because t^3 satisfies X^2-(t^2)^3=0. Thus, by [L2], dim R=dim Z[t^2]=2, not 1.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "b7d04eec710ce75395406bb978f1da16a1dc01faf196366ae71b076936963eab"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:2",
      "id": "ex-steinitz-class-rank-two-module",
      "location": "Example",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "It asserts that M=R⊕I has \"its nonfree rank-one summand\" represented by [I]. Taking the allowed invertible fractional ideal I=R gives M=R⊕R, whose two rank-one summands are free; there is no nonfree rank-one summand.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "82ea08b32348f76c274c18028c9a8c7509ebe04f52793a216dd812faeec68f85"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:3",
      "id": "lem-finite-support-of-ideal-valuations",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "ill-formed",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 chooses \"0≠x∈I\" where I is a fractional ideal I⊂K, then says a prime ideal p⊂R \"contains neither x nor d\" and that \"x/1\" is a unit of R_p. In general x need not lie in R, so membership x∈p and x/1∈R_p are undefined. The usable integral numerator is dx∈R, introduced only in step 2.1.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "2799be543b133b33a28df2a3aac81537b4bfdc066acbb93614fdbd9af1ac5f26"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:4",
      "id": "lem-local-dvr-condition-implies-dimension-one",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "After ruling out a strict prime containment p⊊q with p≠0, the proof concludes \"Therefore every nonzero prime of R is maximal.\" To turn maximal-among-primes into a maximal ideal, p must first be placed in a maximal ideal; the needed result is the declared [L4], \"Every proper ideal ... is contained in a maximal ideal,\" but it is not used in step 1.1.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "9fc18ea5a45d14cc978589dcf55a4bc8f8c6b8ec15a50f9f4ef554ef6559e249"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:5",
      "id": "thm-invertible-ideal-characterisations",
      "location": "Statement (condition 3)",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "Condition 3 lacks finite generation and is false for arbitrary fractional ideals. [Stacks Project §110.32](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CBZ) gives a domain A and a nonzero ideal I⊂A that is not invertible while I_q is principal for every prime q, hence at every maximal ideal; as an integral ideal it is a fractional ideal under the on-disk definition. Thus (3) holds and (1) fails. Correspondingly, step 1.2 silently replaces the localisation of I^{-1}=(R:I) by (R_m:I_m); [L1] establishes only that colon and localisation are well-defined, not that colon commutes with localisation without a finite-generation hypothesis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "1b8ee5b20ef0fd615c9180469d54b00f3c914d727a8dacaad519bda13ce76cd4"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:6",
      "id": "thm-local-characterisation-of-dedekind-domains",
      "location": "1.4",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "In proving (3)⇒(2), the proof starts with an arbitrary nonzero prime p, then says \"Applying (3) at the maximal ideal p.\" Condition (3) applies only to maximal ideals, and no prior step has shown p maximal. Establishing this requires a separate argument via a maximal ideal m⊇p and the local-PID/DVR criterion; the written inference does not license applying (3) at p.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "d86853d3a175c7528b9f68f0da7eae1d57dc12e0606f6cfe164ac619a72779b4"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:7",
      "id": "thm-localisation-of-a-dedekind-domain",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The multiplicative set S is not required to avoid 0. For R=Z and S={0,1}, S^{-1}R is the zero ring, so it is neither a Dedekind domain nor a field; step 1.1's claim that a localisation of a domain is a domain also fails. The cited on-disk dependency thm-noetherian-ring-quotients-and-localisations explicitly records the same boundary: \"0∈S makes S^{-1}R the zero ring.\"",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "387faad78c7f02780f5214f07a4de29e4db3752f0ef8b2167921af41e3b21e95"
    }
  ],
